  "prompt_text": "This schematic illustrates how carbon monoxide oxidizes on a metal surface to form carbon dioxide, highlighting two different product states. The bottom layer of pale spheres represents the catalyst surface, while black and red spheres depict adsorbed CO and O atoms or CO2 intermediates. Red arrows indicate motion of adsorbed oxygen across the surface, suggesting diffusion toward nearby CO species.\n\nThe central idea is that when CO and O meet on the surface they form a bent, surface-bound CO2-like intermediate. From there, the product can leave the surface in different ways. One pathway produces “hyperthermal CO2,” shown as a molecule departing with extra energy, emphasized by wavy lines under the molecule. Other pathways lead to “thermal CO2,” where the molecule appears to desorb more gently after interacting longer with the surface and relaxing its energy.\n\nOverall, the figure compares direct, energetic desorption versus more equilibrated thermal desorption during catalytic CO oxidation. It conveys that the geometry and timing of bond formation and release at the surface control whether the CO2 product leaves hot or thermalized.",
